1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,503
Aarav presses the start
button on his dad's scooter.

2
00:00:03,503 --> 00:00:05,897
For a moment, the headlight goes dim.

3
00:00:06,247 --> 00:00:07,993
The battery is the same.

4
00:00:07,993 --> 00:00:09,320
Nothing is broken.

5
00:00:09,670 --> 00:00:11,965
So where did the missing voltage go?

6
00:00:12,465 --> 00:00:14,560
First, what is a DC source?

7
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:18,884
It is anything that pushes current
in one direction only, with a steady

8
00:00:18,884 --> 00:00:19,784
voltage.

9
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:21,845
A cell makes it from chemicals.

10
00:00:21,845 --> 00:00:24,600
A battery is a group of
cells joined together.

11
00:00:24,950 --> 00:00:27,126
A solar cell makes it from light.

12
00:00:27,126 --> 00:00:30,971
And a DC power supply makes
it from the AC mains at home.

13
00:00:31,321 --> 00:00:35,033
Each one has two terminals,
plus and minus, that never swap.

14
00:00:35,683 --> 00:00:38,329
Every source has an EMF, written E.

15
00:00:38,329 --> 00:00:42,161
It is the voltage the source
gives when no current is drawn.

16
00:00:42,511 --> 00:00:47,512
But inside every real cell, the chemicals
and plates also resist the current.

17
00:00:47,512 --> 00:00:50,343
This is the internal resistance, small r.

18
00:00:50,693 --> 00:00:55,352
So a real source is like a perfect
EMF, with a small resistor hidden

19
00:00:55,352 --> 00:00:56,252
inside it.

20
00:00:56,882 --> 00:00:58,884
Let's test it on the breadboard.

21
00:00:58,884 --> 00:01:02,936
A twelve volt battery, with an
internal resistance of half an ohm.

22
00:01:03,286 --> 00:01:05,962
With the switch open, no current flows.

23
00:01:05,962 --> 00:01:09,307
The voltmeter reads twelve
volts, the full EMF.

24
00:01:09,657 --> 00:01:13,140
Now close the switch, with
a five and a half ohm load.

25
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:14,323
Two amperes flow.

26
00:01:14,673 --> 00:01:16,960
The meter drops to eleven volts.

27
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:20,156
One volt is used up
inside the battery itself.

28
00:01:20,506 --> 00:01:24,237
Now make the load much heavier,
like the scooter's starter motor.

29
00:01:24,237 --> 00:01:26,471
The current shoots up to six amperes.

30
00:01:26,821 --> 00:01:28,105
Watch the scope.

31
00:01:28,105 --> 00:01:32,845
The EMF stays flat, but the terminal
voltage sinks to nine volts.

32
00:01:32,845 --> 00:01:35,100
That is why the headlight dims.

33
00:01:35,750 --> 00:01:37,181
Let's write it down.

34
00:01:37,531 --> 00:01:40,927
Walk around the loop with
Kirchhoff's voltage law.

35
00:01:40,927 --> 00:01:43,906
E minus I r, minus IR, equals zero.

36
00:01:44,606 --> 00:01:48,472
So the current is I equals
E, divided by R plus r.

37
00:01:49,172 --> 00:01:52,721
The terminal voltage is
the drop across the load.

38
00:01:52,721 --> 00:01:53,969
V equals IR.

39
00:01:54,669 --> 00:01:59,261
And since IR is E minus I
r, V equals E minus I r.

40
00:01:59,961 --> 00:02:03,659
I r is the lost voltage,
used up inside the source.

41
00:02:03,659 --> 00:02:06,317
Volts, amperes and ohms, as always.

42
00:02:06,967 --> 00:02:09,508
Now plot V against I for this battery.

43
00:02:09,508 --> 00:02:11,101
It is a straight line.

44
00:02:11,451 --> 00:02:15,044
It starts at E when the current
is zero, and slopes down.

45
00:02:15,044 --> 00:02:17,124
The steepness of the slope is r.

46
00:02:17,474 --> 00:02:21,300
An ideal voltage source has
zero internal resistance.

47
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:22,987
Its line stays flat.

48
00:02:22,987 --> 00:02:24,900
The voltage never drops.

49
00:02:25,250 --> 00:02:27,810
An ideal current source is the opposite.

50
00:02:27,810 --> 00:02:30,475
It pushes the same current into any load.

51
00:02:30,475 --> 00:02:32,345
Its line stands straight up.

52
00:02:32,695 --> 00:02:36,131
Real voltage sources
have a small r in series.

53
00:02:36,131 --> 00:02:40,023
Real current sources have
a very large r in parallel.

54
00:02:40,673 --> 00:02:42,960
EMF is what a source can give.

55
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:45,419
Terminal voltage is what it does give.

56
00:02:46,219 --> 00:02:47,468
Worked example.

57
00:02:47,468 --> 00:02:52,606
A cell of EMF one point five volts
and internal resistance half an ohm

58
00:02:52,606 --> 00:02:55,259
lights a two and a half ohm lamp.

59
00:02:55,609 --> 00:02:58,054
Find the current, and
the terminal voltage.

60
00:02:58,404 --> 00:03:00,563
I equals E over R plus r.

61
00:03:00,563 --> 00:03:02,644
One point five over three.

62
00:03:02,644 --> 00:03:05,129
That is zero point five amperes.

63
00:03:05,829 --> 00:03:10,896
The lost voltage, I r, is zero
point five times zero point five.

64
00:03:10,896 --> 00:03:13,012
Zero point two five volts.

65
00:03:13,712 --> 00:03:17,704
So V equals one point five
minus zero point two five.

66
00:03:17,704 --> 00:03:19,723
One point two five volts.

67
00:03:20,373 --> 00:03:21,838
Now, the exam trap.

68
00:03:22,188 --> 00:03:26,659
Many students think the terminal
voltage always equals the EMF.

69
00:03:27,009 --> 00:03:28,982
Only when no current flows.

70
00:03:28,982 --> 00:03:32,524
When a cell gives current,
V equals E minus I r.

71
00:03:32,524 --> 00:03:35,962
When it is being charged,
V equals E plus I r.

72
00:03:36,612 --> 00:03:38,251
Where is this asked?

73
00:03:38,251 --> 00:03:42,256
In PUC board papers, as
definitions and short problems.

74
00:03:42,256 --> 00:03:47,615
In JEE Main and NEET, as terminal voltage
and internal resistance questions.

75
00:03:48,265 --> 00:03:49,165
Try this.

76
00:03:49,139 --> 00:03:54,130
A battery reads eleven volts at its
terminals when it supplies two amperes,

77
00:03:54,130 --> 00:03:57,255
and ten volts when it
supplies four amperes.

78
00:03:57,255 --> 00:04:00,113
Find its EMF and internal resistance.

79
00:04:05,513 --> 00:04:06,413
A.

80
00:04:05,934 --> 00:04:08,049
Twelve volts and half an ohm.

81
00:04:08,399 --> 00:04:12,014
Use V equals E minus I
r, for both readings.

82
00:04:12,364 --> 00:04:14,749
Eleven equals E minus two r.

83
00:04:14,749 --> 00:04:17,324
And ten equals E minus four r.

84
00:04:17,674 --> 00:04:19,047
Subtract the two.

85
00:04:19,047 --> 00:04:22,105
One equals two r, so r is half an ohm.

86
00:04:22,455 --> 00:04:25,432
Then E equals eleven, plus two times half.

87
00:04:25,432 --> 00:04:26,468
Twelve volts.

88
00:04:27,118 --> 00:04:28,639
Screenshot this card.

89
00:04:31,839 --> 00:04:34,637
Next, network theorems
and Thevenin's theorem.

90
00:04:34,637 --> 00:04:38,579
How to shrink a whole circuit
into one battery and one resistor.
